data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"command\": \"mkdir -p ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/report.md \u003c\u003c'EOF'\\n# Campaign csrclinh — final report\\n\\n## Mission\\n\\\"greet, tested\\\": deliver src/greet.py with greet(name) returning\\n\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\" and tests/test_greet.py covering a normal name and the empty\\nstring, with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exiting 0.\\n\\n## What happened\\n- Sent dev a single dispatch (d002) with the exact deliverable spec.\\n- dev implemented src/greet.py and tests/test_greet.py and committed to its\\n  own branch (cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>, commit c5729ad on\\n  base 1053761), reporting a passing local test run.\\n- Judged independently rather than trusting the report: fetched dev's branch,\\n  diffed it against base (confirmed only the two intended files were added,\\n  no scope creep), checked out the commit in an isolated git worktree, and\\n  ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself — 2 tests, OK, exit 0.\\n- Accepted d002.\\n\\n## Outcome\\nAll three stated criteria are satisfied:\\n1. src/greet.py defines greet(name) -\u003e \\\"Hello, {name}!\\\".\\n2. tests/test_greet.py covers greet(\\\"Alice\\\") and greet(\\\"\\\").\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 (verified independently).\\n\\nMission met on the first attempt; no rework was needed.\\nEOF\\necho written\", \"description\": \"Write durable final report to campaign output directory\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

